<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    WPR.Engine.Input - the input-emulation subsystem: what turns a head's IKeyboardEmulationHost
    (the module that knows what a key MEANS) into XNA-visible input for the running game.

      * KeyboardEmulation        - attaches the two components below to a freshly created Game,
                                   and wraps the platform's IInputBackend in the touch injector.
      * TiltInputXnaComponent    - polls Keyboard.GetState each Update and reports held keys plus
                                   the presented orientation to the host.
      * TiltOverlayXnaComponent  - the optional on-screen tilt dial.
      * SyntheticTouchInputBackend - the IInputBackend decorator that writes a synthesised finger
                                   into TouchPanel after the platform drain (see its remarks for
                                   why it must be a decorator and not a component).

    Moved here from WPR.Backend.FNA/Input/ on 2026-09-20. They were put in the backend on
    2026-09-01 because "they derive from spine types and therefore have to live in a backend" -
    which was true for the two days before the spine (Game, GameComponent,
    DrawableGameComponent) moved into WPR.Framework.Xna. Since then nothing in these four files
    has named an FNA type: they read Keyboard, TouchPanel, SpriteBatch and GameWindow, all of
    which are the framework's. A component that derives from a framework type is framework-tier
    code composed by the engine, not backend code.

    The seam this implements against (WPR.Xna.Rhi.IKeyboardEmulationHost) stays in the framework
    because it speaks Keys and DisplayOrientation; the implementation of THAT is the
    WPR.Input.Keyboard module. So the layering reads: module (meaning of a key) -> this project
    (how it reaches the game) -> framework (the devices the game polls).

    References the framework's internals - TouchPanel.SetFinger / INTERNAL_onTouchEvent / the
    reserved-slot bookkeeping, and GameWindow.HostClientBounds - through an InternalsVisibleTo
    in WPR.Framework.Xna.csproj, the same arrangement the backend had.

    No FNA reference, and BackendIsolationTests scans Engine/ to keep it that way.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup Condition=" '$(OS)' != 'Windows_NT' ">
    <CheckEolWorkloads>false</CheckEolWorkloads>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>net10.0;net10.0-android</TargetFrameworks>
    <CheckEolWorkloads>false</CheckEolWorkloads>
    <AssemblyName>WPR.Engine.Input</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>WPR.Engine.Input</RootNamespace>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <SupportedOSPlatformVersion Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net10.0-android'">24.0</SupportedOSPlatformVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Game / GameComponent / Keyboard / TouchPanel / SpriteBatch and the WPR.Xna.Rhi seams. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="$(SolutionDir)Core\WPR.Framework.Xna\WPR.Framework.Xna.csproj" />
    <!-- Log.Warn when attaching the components fails. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="$(SolutionDir)Core\WPR.Common\WPR.Common.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
